  "full_response": "### **What is a Service Mesh?**\nA **service mesh** is a dedicated infrastructure layer that manages service-to-service communication within a microservices architecture. It provides a transparent and language-agnostic way to handle networking concerns such as:\n\n- **Service Discovery** – Locating services dynamically.\n- **Load Balancing** – Distributing traffic across service instances.\n- **Traffic Management** – Routing, retries, timeouts, and circuit breaking.\n- **Security** – Encryption (mTLS), authentication, and authorization.\n- **Observability** – Metrics, logging, and distributed tracing.\n- **Resilience** – Handling failures gracefully (retries, fallbacks).\n\nInstead of implementing these features in each microservice, a service mesh offloads them to a **sidecar proxy** (e.g., Envoy, Linkerd) that runs alongside each service instance.\n\n---\n\n### **Why Use a Service Mesh in Microservices?**\nMicroservices introduce complexity in networking, security, and observability. A service mesh helps by:\n\n#### **1. Decoupling Networking Logic from Business Logic**\n- Developers focus on application code while the service mesh handles networking.\n- No need to rewrite networking logic when switching languages or frameworks.\n\n#### **2. Improving Resilience & Fault Tolerance**\n- **Circuit Breaking** – Prevents cascading failures by stopping requests to unhealthy services.\n- **Retries & Timeouts** – Automatically retries failed requests with configurable delays.\n- **Rate Limiting** – Protects services from being overwhelmed.\n\n#### **3. Enhancing Security**\n- **mTLS (Mutual TLS)** – Encrypts all service-to-service traffic by default.\n- **Fine-Grained Access Control** – Enforces policies (e.g., \"Service A can only call Service B\").\n- **Identity & Authentication** – Uses SPIFFE/SPIRE for service identity.\n\n#### **4. Simplifying Observability**\n- **Metrics** – Tracks latency, error rates, and request volumes (Prometheus, Grafana).\n- **Distributed Tracing** – Follows requests across services (Jaeger, Zipkin).\n- **Logging** – Centralizes logs for debugging.\n\n#### **5. Enabling Advanced Traffic Management**\n- **Canary Deployments** – Gradually roll out new versions to a subset of users.\n- **A/B Testing** – Route traffic based on headers or user segments.\n- **Blue-Green",
